---
import BaseLayout from "../../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="mdbase Reader extension privacy"
  description="How the mdbase Reader browser extension handles page content, annotations, browsing addresses, and Connect authorization."
>
  <article class="legal">
    <header>
      <h1>mdbase Reader browser extension privacy</h1>
      <p>
        This page covers the mdbase Reader browser extension, operated by
        Callum Alpass. The <a href="/privacy/">mdbase website and Connect privacy page</a>
        covers the related website and Connect service.
      </p>
    </header>

    <h2>What the extension does</h2>
    <p>
      Reader lets you save supported web articles and PDFs, add highlights and
      notes, and revisit them in a collection you authorize through mdbase
      Connect. The extension is not a standalone storage service.
    </p>

    <h2>Pages and information you choose to save</h2>
    <p>
      When you open Reader on a supported page, it reads the page address,
      title, content, available citation metadata and selected text to prepare
      a capture. Saving is a separate action. When you save, Reader transfers
      the source content and any tags, notes, comments or highlights you enter
      through Connect to your selected collection.
    </p>
    <p>
      For articles, new captures include a readable copy and a minimized
      text-and-structure HTML archive. The archive leaves out scripts, forms,
      embedded application state, arbitrary attributes and resource or link
      addresses from the document markup. Explicitly hidden elements are
      removed. This is not anonymization: visible text, titles, citation
      metadata and the separately saved source address, including any query
      parameters, may still contain private information. Existing captures are
      not rewritten. Only save pages you intend to keep in that collection.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you save a supported PDF, Reader downloads the document from
      within the page, using your existing cookies and login for that
      website. This lets Reader save PDFs that are available to you only
      while signed in. If Reader cannot fetch it from the page, the
      extension tries to download the PDF directly. The PDF goes only to your
      selected collection. Reader does not export or save the website's cookies or
      your login details.
    </p>

    <h2>Citation lookups</h2>
    <p>
      If a DOI is available, citation preparation may send that DOI to{" "}
      <code>doi.org</code> and the registry it redirects to, such as Crossref
      or DataCite. This can happen before you save. The lookup does not
      intentionally send your notes, highlights or full article content.
      Those services also receive ordinary network information such as your IP
      address. Extension DOI requests omit cookies.
    </p>
    <p>
      Wikimedia Citoid lookup is off by default. If you turn on “Look up
      citations with Wikimedia’s Citoid service” in extension Settings and no
      DOI citation is found for a web page, Reader sends the captured page's
      address to Wikimedia's Citoid service. Reader does not send addresses
      of PDFs, local or private hostnames, IP addresses, or links whose query
      parameters look like tokens, keys or session codes. Citoid requests omit
      cookies and other credentials. Like DOI lookups, this can happen before
      you save.
    </p>

    <h2>Website access and saved-page recognition</h2>
    <p>
      Reader requests access to all HTTPS websites when you install it. With
      that access, the side panel follows the tab you are on, and Reader marks
      pages you have saved and shows your highlights on them. To do this,
      Reader queries your selected collection through your configured Connect
      route. It keeps short hashes of the addresses saved in that collection
      in extension storage and refreshes them from the collection about once
      an hour. When an HTTPS page you load matches one of them, Reader sends
      the page's address to your collection to retrieve its saved highlights,
      and may read page text to display them. While the side panel is open,
      Reader also sends the address of the tab you are on to your collection
      to check whether that page is saved.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can limit Reader's site access in Chrome's extension settings. Reader
      then stops following your tabs and marking saved pages, and works on a
      page only where you have allowed it or after you click its toolbar
      button there. The “Pages you visit” section of extension Settings shows
      when site access is limited and can ask Chrome to allow it on all sites
      again.
    </p>

    <h2>Connection and local browser data</h2>
    <p>
      The extension keeps Connect authorization state and grants, the Connect
      account email it shows, the selected collection, preferences, the hashed
      saved-page addresses described above and interrupted-write recovery
      information in Chrome's extension-local storage and the extension's
      IndexedDB databases. Signing keys and application identity are held in
      IndexedDB. Recovery data can include identifiers, addresses and pending
      changes. Draft text and selected passages are kept per tab in extension
      session storage and cleaned up when their tab closes. The extension does
      not use Chrome's synchronized storage.
    </p>
    <p>
      Connect grants are sensitive credentials used to access only collections
      you authorize. Extension API requests do not use ambient Connect portal
      cookies. The separate Connect approval website handles its own sign-in.
    </p>

    <h2>Where data goes</h2>
    <p>
      The production extension contacts <code>connect.mdbase.dev</code> for
      authorization and collection operations. The mdbase SDK in the extension
      also contacts <code>log.mdbase.dev</code>, mdbase's first-party
      collection log and file transport. It carries the operations on
      collections you have authorized and is not an analytics service.
      Depending on your collection, records and files are handled by the readable copy mdbase keeps (a Cloud
      copy collection) or by one of your own devices through the mdbase relay
      (a Private collection). Not all traffic remains on your computer. The{" "}
      <a href="/privacy/">Connect privacy page</a> describes account, routing
      and Cloud copy handling.
    </p>
    <p>
      Websites you capture, DOI registries and, if you turn it on, Wikimedia's
      Citoid service have their own privacy practices.
    </p>

    <h2>Purposes, service providers and sharing</h2>
    <p>
      Reader uses this information to prepare and save sources, look up citation
      metadata, store annotations, access an authorized collection, recover
      interrupted writes and recognize saved pages. Connect
      processes account and grant details, routing metadata and bounded usage
      counts needed to operate the service; its operator report uses aggregate
      counts rather than returning individual records or page contents.
    </p>
    <p>
      Production Connect runs on Render with managed PostgreSQL. Collection
      logs, snapshots and files are stored encrypted with Cloudflare, in
      Durable Objects and R2. For a Cloud copy collection, mdbase's readable copy runs
      on Cloudflare and handles authorized record and file operations.
      Render provides database recovery, and encrypted logical database
      backup artifacts are retained through GitHub Actions. Account email may
      be delivered through Resend. DOI registries receive a DOI when
      a lookup is made, Wikimedia receives a page address when you have
      turned on Citoid lookup, and the original website serves content or a
      PDF when you choose to capture it. These providers may process the network and
      operational information necessary to provide those services. The
      extension does not contain an advertising or third-party analytics SDK.
      We do not sell user data, use or transfer it for purposes unrelated to
      Reader's stated function, or use or transfer it to determine
      creditworthiness or for lending. Transfers to service providers, DOI
      registries and Wikimedia are limited to the functions described above.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our use of information received from Google APIs adheres to the
      <a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/webstore/program-policies/limited-use/">Chrome Web Store User Data Policy, including the Limited Use requirements</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Retention and controls</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Saved sources, files and annotations remain in the selected collection
        until removed using the collection's controls. Removing the extension
        does not delete them.
      </li>
      <li>
        Session drafts are temporary. Local preferences, authorization and
        recovery state can persist across browser restarts.
      </li>
      <li>
        Limit Reader's site access in Chrome's extension settings to stop
        background saved-page lookups for the pages you visit.
      </li>
      <li>
        Removing the extension from Chrome deletes its extension-local
        storage, session drafts and IndexedDB data, including its Connect
        credentials, in that browser. Finish pending saves first: a write
        already sent may still complete, and recovery information is discarded.
      </li>
      <li>
        Removing the extension does not revoke server-side grants or delete
        collection content, and does not affect other browsers. Highlights
        already drawn on an open page may remain until it is reloaded. Revoke
        Reader's application access in Connect and remove records/files in
        your collection using its controls. Revocation for a collection served
        by one of your devices can remain pending until that device confirms it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Connect deletes expired authorization and usage rows that are no longer
      needed, generally after about 13 months. Other account, grant and audit
      information can be retained while needed for service, security or legal
      purposes. Deleting a file from a synced collection may first leave
      retained versions and backups that are removed through maintenance or
      backup expiry; deletion from all recovery copies is not immediate.
      Cloudflare and Render may retain operational logs. The{" "}
      <a href="/account-deletion/">Connect account-deletion page</a> explains
      account-level controls and exceptions; collection files on your own
      devices are not removed by deleting an account.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact and changes</h2>
    <p>
      For privacy questions or requests, contact
      <a href="mailto:callum@mdbase.dev">Callum Alpass at callum@mdbase.dev</a>.
      Changes to this policy will be posted at this address with an updated
      effective date.
    </p>
    <p>Effective date: 2 November 2026.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
